\section*{1. Architectural Overview}
This architecture defines a next-generation secure cryptographic coprocessor
integrating:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Physical Unclonable Functions (PUFs),
  \item post-quantum cryptographic acceleration,
  \item hardware-enforced memory enclaves,
  \item encrypted internal buses,
  \item side-channel-hardened execution units,
  \item autonomous telemetry and ledger hashing.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{1.1 PUF Core}
A silicon-entropy Physical Unclonable Function generates a non-cloneable
device root key:
\[
K_{\text{root}} = \text{PUF}(\text{silicon\_entropy})
\]
No non-volatile storage is used.

\subsection*{1.2 Post-Quantum Cryptographic Engine}
Hardware pipelines implement:
\begin{itemize}
  \item CRYSTALS-Kyber (KEM),
  \item CRYSTALS-Dilithium (signatures).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{1.3 Secure Enclave Memory Controller}
The SEMC enforces hardware-isolated memory zones using:
\begin{itemize}
  \item cryptographic address scrambling,
  \item inline memory encryption (IME),
  \item bus-level access control.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{1.4 Autonomous Telemetry \& Ledger Hashing}
A real-time hashing pipeline commits hardware telemetry and state transitions
to an append-only verification ledger.

\section*{2. Hardware Implementation Blueprint}

\subsection*{2.1 Core Pipeline}
\[
\text{PUF Core} \rightarrow \text{KDF} \rightarrow \text{Enclave Controller}
\]

\[
